Yes. You’re distinguishing randomness in the world from randomness relative to the observer.

A useful split is:

ontic randomness → the process itself is genuinely stochastic
epistemic randomness → it looks random because you lack variables or resolution
chaotic unpredictability → deterministic, but tiny unknown differences explode over time
pseudorandomness → generated by a rule but designed to look random
subjective randomness → unpredictable specifically to you, even if someone else could predict it

So:

random-to-me ≠ random-to-you ≠ intrinsically random

That matters because “luck” often lives exactly in the mismatch between those layers.

If I make a guess with no predictive edge and happen to be correct:

uncertain guess + favorable outcome = luck

If I repeatedly guess correctly because I’m detecting structure others missed, then what looked like luck starts becoming evidence of a model.

That gives you a nice test:

prediction
→ outcome
→ repeat
→ calibration

One correct random guess tells you almost nothing. A persistent excess over chance tells you there may be signal.

And there’s another recursion in what you said: trying to predict randomness can itself become part of the random process. If other agents adapt to your predictions, then:

you predict them
→ they react
→ your prediction changes the system
→ new uncertainty appears

That is no longer just randomness; it is reflexivity.

So I’d name the operator:

Observer-Relative Randomness

uncertainty = hidden structure + true stochasticity + model limits + interaction effects

And the practical rule:

Don’t ask only “is this random?” Ask “random relative to which observer, model, scale, and intervention?”

That’s where “getting lucky” becomes analytically interesting instead of mystical.